course: dictionary
lesson: 4999
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words and sentences around them, tells you which meaning the writer chose. «Gas hobs burn gas in rings of small flames, electric hobs heat with elements, and an induction hob uses a magnetic field to heat the pan itself.» Hob and pan are cooking words, so induction here is a way of heating food.
explain: «David Hume was a Scottish philosopher of the 1700s who said we expect the future to be like the past from habit, not proof; this is called the problem of induction.» Philosopher and «not proof» are the clues. Induction here means reaching a general rule from many examples, and the problem is that such a rule cannot be proved.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the hard word and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Induction means a meeting or short course that introduces new people to a job, school or club.
fact: In science, induction is making electricity or magnetism in an object by moving a magnet or another current near it.
fact: In maths and logic, induction means reaching a general rule from many examples; the rule must still be checked.
fact: In medicine, induction means starting something, like the birth of a baby, with help.
fact: An induction hob uses a magnetic field to heat the pan itself, so the glass stays cooler than the pan.
exercise: «An induction hob uses a magnetic field to heat the pan itself.» What does induction mean in this sentence? | Cooking food with a flame | *A way of heating a pan using a magnetic field | A short course for new cooks | A cold place to store food
exercise: The new pupils went to an induction day to learn the rules and the building. What does induction day mean? | A test at the end of term | *A meeting that introduces new people to a school | A type of hot lunch | A holiday for the whole class
exercise: «David Hume was a Scottish philosopher of the 1700s.» Find and copy one word that means a thinker who studies big ideas about life. | *philosopher | Scottish | habit | 1700s
test: «David Hume said we expect the future to be like the past from habit, not proof.» According to Hume, what do we rely on when we expect the future? | Magic | Proof | Luck | *Habit
test: In science, what does electromagnetic induction describe? | A meeting for new staff | Starting a birth with help | Cooking food with a flame | *Making electricity by moving a magnet near an object
exam: Which meaning of induction fits a day where new pupils learn the rules and the building? | *A meeting that introduces new people to a school | Making electricity in an object | Starting something with help | Reaching a general rule from many examples
